// The prelude — the one namespace in `core` that is in scope without an import.
//
// The compiler globs this namespace's `@public` members into every file's
// outermost scope (§4.6). **Nothing else in `core` is globbed**: `Add`,
// `Iterator`, `Ordering` and the rest need `import <core/ops>` and friends, like
// any other package's names.
//
// Membership is decided by one question: *does a program that writes no imports
// at all need this name to be readable?* `Option`, `Result` and `ControlFlow`
// appear in signatures everywhere and two of them are what `.?` / `.!` desugar
// to; `str` is the type of every string literal. The operator traits fail the
// test — `a + b` finds `Add` by its `#lang` tag, never by name, so the name is
// needed only in order to *write* an impl, which is a deliberate act that can
// afford a line of import.
//
// Globbing all of `core` instead would make every name core ever adds a name
// that can collide with a program's own, in every file, forever. That is why a
// prelude is small in every language that has one.
//
// This file only re-exports; every member is declared in the topic file that
// owns it. The compiler finds this namespace by the `#lang("prelude")` tag on
// the binding in `core.nest`, never by name or by path, so a `core` laid out
// differently still works.

@public { Option } :: import "option.nest"
@public { Result } :: import "result.nest"
@public { ControlFlow } :: import "control.nest"
@public { str } :: import "str.nest"

// `usize` / `isize` are ordinary declarations in `num.nest` (§3.1), not compiler
// primitives, so the prelude is what makes them nameable without an import — the
// same way `str` is.
@public { usize, isize } :: import "num.nest"

// The three intrinsics §6.4 puts here. `cast` appears wherever a conversion is
// written, `panic` wherever something must not happen, and `size_of` in enough
// signatures and constants to earn its place; the rest of `core/mem` and all of
// `core/gc` are an import away.
@public { cast, size_of } :: import "mem.nest"
@public { panic } :: import "fail.nest"
